============
Git commands
============

Here's a comprehensive Git commands reference:

Setup & Configuration
---------------------
Command	Explanation
---------------------
git init	Initialize a new Git repository in the current folder
git clone <url>	Download a remote repository to your local machine
git config --global user.name "Name"	Set your name for all repositories
git config --global user.email "email"	Set your email for all repositories
git config --list	Show all Git configuration settings
Basic Workflow (Daily Use)

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git status	Show which files are modified, staged, or untracked
git add <file>	Stage a specific file for commit
git add .	Stage all changed and new files
git add -A	Stage everything (new, modified, and deleted files)
git commit -m "message"	Save staged changes with a descriptive message
git commit --amend	Modify the last commit (message or add files)
git diff	Show unstaged changes (what you haven't added yet)
git diff --staged	Show staged changes (what will be committed)
Branching

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git branch	List all local branches
git branch <name>	Create a new branch
git branch -d <name>	Delete a branch (safe, won't delete unmerged work)
git branch -D <name>	Force delete a branch (even if unmerged)
git branch -a	List all branches (local + remote)
git checkout <branch>	Switch to an existing branch
git checkout -b <name>	Create and switch to a new branch
git switch <branch>	Switch branches (modern alternative to checkout)
git switch -c <name>	Create and switch to a new branch (modern)
Merging & Rebasing

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git merge <branch>	Merge another branch into your current branch
git merge --abort	Cancel a merge that has conflicts
git rebase <branch>	Reapply your commits on top of another branch
git rebase --abort	Cancel an in-progress rebase
git rebase --continue	Continue rebase after resolving conflicts
git cherry-pick <commit>	Apply a specific commit from another branch
Remote Repositories

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git remote -v	Show all remote repositories and their URLs
git remote add origin <url>	Connect your local repo to a remote
git remote set-url origin <url>	Change the remote URL
git remote remove <name>	Remove a remote connection
git fetch	Download remote changes without merging them
git fetch --all	Fetch from all remotes
git pull	Fetch + merge remote changes into your branch
git pull --rebase	Fetch + rebase (cleaner history, no merge commits)
git push	Upload your commits to the remote
git push -u origin <branch>	Push and set upstream tracking for a new branch
git push --force	Force overwrite remote (dangerous, use carefully)
git push --force-with-lease	Safer force push (fails if remote has new commits)
git push origin --delete <branch>	Delete a remote branch
Viewing History

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git log	Show full commit history
git log --oneline	Compact log (one commit per line)
git log --graph	Show branch/merge history visually
git log --oneline --graph --all	Visual overview of all branches
git log -n 5	Show only the last 5 commits
git log --author="Name"	Filter commits by author
git log <file>	Show commit history for a specific file
git show <commit>	Show details and diff of a specific commit
git blame <file>	Show who last modified each line of a file
Undoing Changes

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git restore <file>	Discard changes in a file (revert to last commit)
git restore --staged <file>	Unstage a file (keep the changes, remove from staging)
git reset HEAD~1	Undo last commit, keep changes in working directory
git reset --soft HEAD~1	Undo last commit, keep changes staged
git reset --hard HEAD~1	Undo last commit and discard all changes (dangerous)
git revert <commit>	Create a new commit that undoes a specific commit (safe)
git clean -fd	Remove untracked files and directories (dangerous)
git clean -n	Preview what clean would delete (dry run)
Stashing (Temporary Storage)

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git stash	Save uncommitted changes and clean working directory
git stash save "message"	Stash with a descriptive name
git stash list	Show all stashed changes
git stash pop	Apply most recent stash and remove it from stash list
git stash apply	Apply most recent stash but keep it in stash list
git stash drop	Delete the most recent stash
git stash clear	Delete all stashes
Tagging (Versioning)

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git tag	List all tags
git tag v1.0.0	Create a lightweight tag
git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "Release 1.0"	Create an annotated tag with message
git push origin v1.0.0	Push a specific tag to remote
git push origin --tags	Push all tags to remote
git tag -d v1.0.0	Delete a local tag
Inspection & Debugging

Command	Explanation
---------------------
git reflog	Show history of all HEAD movements (recov
ery tool)
git bisect start	Start binary search to find a bug-introducing commit
git bisect good <commit>	Mark a commit as "good" (no bug)
git bisect bad <commit>	Mark a commit as "bad" (has the bug)
git bisect reset	End bisect session
git shortlog -s -n	Show commit count per author
git whatchanged	Show what files changed in each commit
Advanced / Less Common
Command	Explanation
git submodule add <url>	Add another repo inside your repo
git submodule update --init	Initialize and fetch submodule content
git archive --format=zip HEAD > project.zip	Export repo as a zip file
git gc	Garbage collect — optimize repo storage
git fsck	Check repo integrity for corruption
git worktree add <path> <branch>	Check out a branch in a separate directory
Common Workflows (Combos)
powershell

# Start a new feature
git checkout -b feature/login-page

# Save your work
git add .
git commit -m "feat: add login page"

# Push to remote
git push -u origin feature/login-page

# Update your branch with latest main
git checkout main
git pull
git checkout feature/login-page
git merge main

# After merge conflicts are resolved
git add .
git commit -m "fix: resolve merge conflicts"

# Delete branch after merge
git branch -d feature/login-page
git push origin --delete feature/login-page
